# Dispatch in-flight supremacy

**Invariant:** `dispatch_active(slice) ∧ overlap(in_seat_work, slice) ⇒ halt(in_seat) ∨ operator_authorizes_override`.

When a `team_dispatch(op=generate, seat=cursor-sdk, …)` (or nested cursor-sdk leg) is
**in flight** for a bounded slice, the IDE lead seat **must not** implement that same
slice in the shared checkout — poll, harvest, adjudicate, and orchestrate only —
unless the operator **explicitly authorizes** override.

**Lane note:** default cursor-sdk generate runs on an isolated Lane-B worktree, not
the shared checkout. In-flight supremacy still blocks overlapping in-seat edits on
the **same paths** whether the worker holds Lane B or Lane A.

Sole shared `master` + parallel writers on the same paths is the failure class this
blocks (friction from agent-bus:6486 / 6488, 2026-07-30).

## Dispatch active (detect before any edit)

Treat dispatch as **active** when **ANY**:

| Signal | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `team_dispatch` returned `execution_id` / `dispatch_id` and `terminal` is not true | Worker admitted, not closed |
| `poll_hint` exists for `from_agent=cursor-sdk` on a dispatch thread | Await harvest, do not duplicate |
| `agent_bus` thread shows SDK generate admitted without terminal closeout JSON | Still running |
| Scoreboard / thread state marks slice **IN FLIGHT** for cursor-sdk | Standing commission |

**Overlap** = same `source_ref`, `packet_path`, `files_expected` loci, or charter G-row /
todo slug the dispatch was commissioned for.

## Forbidden without operator authorization

| Action | Why |
|---|---|
| Edit files the in-flight dispatch owns (`files_expected`, packet scope, same todo) | Parallel writer on shared checkout |
| “User said Go / Composer” as license to re-implement an already-commissioned slice | Seat identity ≠ executor swap mid-flight |
| Start in-seat implement because SDK poll is slow | Latency ≠ authorization |
| Partial in-seat head start “while SDK runs” | Same overlap class |

## Permitted in-seat (no override needed)

| Action | When |
|---|---|
| Poll `agent_bus.wait` / read dispatch thread / harvest closeout | Default while active |
| Author spec or packet **before** firing dispatch | Pre-commission only |
| Edits **outside** dispatch overlap (different todo, different paths, no shared files) | No parallel writer on same loci |
| Lead verify **after** terminal closeout (read-back, tests on reported paths) | Post-harvest only |
| Abort/cancel dispatch | **Operator authorization required** — `session-abort-authorization_ulg` |

## Operator authorization (explicit only)

Override is allowed only when the operator **names** the override, e.g.:

- “abort the dispatch” / “cancel 6488” / “kill execution …”
- “implement in IDE, don’t wait for SDK”
- “override dispatch — in-seat only”

`¬` infer authorization from tier change (“you are Composer”), urgency, or impatience.

If authorized to override: **still** do not silently leave the SDK worker writing —
abort or accept explicit parallel-WIP reconciliation per `git-posture` / shared-checkout.

## Procedure (binding)

1. Before mechanical edits on a commissioned slice: check dispatch-active signals.
2. If active ∧ overlap → **poll/harvest**; do not edit overlapping paths.
3. If operator authorizes override → announce which dispatch/thread is superseded; then in-seat or abort per their words.
4. After closeout → verify worker artifacts; reconcile dirty tree; do not assume in-seat edits “won.”

## Anti-patterns

| Bad | Good |
|---|---|
| Fire SDK dispatch, then implement same S1 in IDE because seat is Composer | Poll 6488; harvest; verify |
| “I’ll get a head start while SDK runs” | Wait for terminal closeout or get override |
| Edit `ledger_age.py` while SDK packet lists `ledger_age.py` | Orchestrate until harvest |
| Assume slow poll = dead dispatch | Read thread + execution status |

## Composes with

- `lean-context-dispatch-first` — dispatch is default; this rule guards **after** dispatch fires
- `bind-then-compose-dispatch` — compose leg stays cursor-sdk; lead does not steal compose
- `session-abort-authorization` — abort is operator-gated; override ≠ silent abort
- `shared-checkout-housekeeping` — parallel dirty paths are WIP, not disposable
- `git-posture` — sole checkout, no intersecting writers
